                 and the Visium HD continuous lawn of 2 x 2 um barcoded squares
                 binned to 8 x 8 um. Vendor document: re-check on each chemistry
                 release, as these numbers move.}
}

@article{spot_deconvolution_cells_per_spot,
  title   = {Single-cell level deconvolution, convolution, and clustering in
             spatial transcriptomics},
  journal = {Nature Communications},
  year    = {2023},
  note    = {PMC10769305. Cited for simulated median cells per 55 um spot by
             tissue: mouse kidney 19, mouse brain cortex 6, hippocampus 5, human
             breast cancer 6; and for the unsampled-area calculation. Read:
             abstract and results.}
}

@article{xenium_quality_2025,
  title   = {Optimizing Xenium In Situ data utility by quality assessment and
             best-practice analysis workflows},
  journal = {Nature Methods},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41592-025-02617-2},
  note    = {Cited for Xenium detection efficiency 1.2-1.5x that of Chromium v2
             scRNA-seq depending on metric and region, and for the observation
             that commercial platforms converge on similar detection efficiency.}
}

@article{ist_benchmark_2025,
  title   = {Systematic benchmarking of imaging spatial transcriptomics platforms},
  journal = {Nature Communications},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41467-025-64990-y},
  note    = {Xenium, MERSCOPE and CosMx across a tumour tissue microarray. Cited
             for platform-dependent FDR (Xenium lowest in 15 of 22 TMA-cancer
             combinations) and for concordance with bulk RNA-seq. Note the
             chemistry-version dependence: 2023 and 2024 runs differ substantially.}
}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- segmentation

@article{baysor_2022,
  title   = {Cell segmentation in imaging-based spatial transcriptomics},
  journal = {Nature Biotechnology},
  year    = {2022},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41587-021-01044-w},
  note    = {Baysor. Cited for transcript-composition-based segmentation and the
             claim that it can nearly double recovered cell counts while reducing
             contamination. Read alongside proseg_2025, which reports the same
             behaviour as systematic over-segmentation.}
}

@article{proseg_2025,
  title   = {Cell simulation as cell segmentation},
  year    = {2025},
  note    = {Proseg. PMID 40404994. Cited for the spurious-co-expression metric:
             gene pairs whose co-expression rises sharply as nuclear boundaries
             are expanded. Also reports Baysor predicting substantially more cells
             than alternatives, read as systematic over-segmentation.}
}

@article{comseg_2024,
  title   = {A point cloud segmentation framework for image-based spatial
             transcriptomics},
  journal = {Communications Biology},
  year    = {2024},
  doi     = {10.1038/s42003-024-06480-3},
  note    = {ComSeg. Cited for cell-type calling accuracy against simulated ground
             truth: ComSeg 74 percent, Watershed 60, pciSeq 62, SCS 35, Baysor 62.
             This is the strongest available quantification of how much the
             segmentation choice moves the downstream answer.}
}

@article{crisp_2026_preprint,
  title   = {CRISP enables comparisons of image-based spatial transcriptomic
             segmentation methods},
  year    = {2026},
  note    = {PREPRINT, bioRxiv, not peer reviewed at time of citation. Measures
             segmentation purity via co-expression of mutually exclusive markers,
             without ground truth. Cited for the capture-versus-purity tradeoff
             and its tissue dependence. Re-check for a published version.}
}

% ---------------------------------------------------------------- inference

@article{svg_benchmark_2025,
  title   = {Systematic benchmarking of computational methods to identify
             spatially variable genes},
  journal = {Genome Biology},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1186/s13059-025-03731-2},
  note    = {Cited for the central finding that most SVG methods produce poorly
             calibrated p-values, and for the resulting recommendation to select
             SVGs by a fixed rank threshold rather than by significance.}
}

@article{nnsvg_2023,
  title   = {nnSVG for the scalable identification of spatially variable genes
             using nearest-neighbor Gaussian processes},
  journal = {Nature Communications},
  year    = {2023},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41467-023-39748-z},
  note    = {Cited as a counterexample to general miscalibration: reports false
             positive proportions of 1.5 and 1.0 percent at a nominal 0.05 cutoff
             in two null simulations, i.e. conservative rather than
             anticonservative.}
}

@article{gcea_false_positives_2021,
  title   = {Overcoming false-positive gene-category enrichment in the analysis of
             spatially resolved transcriptomic brain atlas data},
  journal = {Nature Communications},
  year    = {2021},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41467-021-22862-1},
  note    = {Cited for over 500-fold average inflation of false-positive GO
             category associations with random spatially autocorrelated
             phenotypes, and for ensemble-based null models that randomise the
             phenotype rather than the genes. Brain atlas data, so generalisation
             to tissue sections is an inference, not a finding.}
}

@article{st_de_typeI_2025_preprint,
  title   = {A comparative study of statistical methods for identifying
             differentially expressed genes in spatial transcriptomics},
  year    = {2025},
  note    = {PREPRINT, bioRxiv, not peer reviewed at time of citation. Cited for
             inflated Type I error of the Wilcoxon rank-sum test under strong
             spatial correlation.}
}

% ------------------------------------------------- batch 2: data generation

@article{omics_design_2025,
  title   = {How thoughtful experimental design can empower biologists in the
             omics era},
  journal = {Nature Communications},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41467-025-62616-x},
  note    = {Cited for the definition of pseudoreplication, the independence
             requirement for replicates, the misconception that data quantity
             substitutes for biological replication, and randomisation as
             protection against confounding. General experimental design, applied
             here to spatial data rather than derived from it.}
}

@article{powerest_2025,
  title   = {PoweREST: Statistical power estimation for spatial transcriptomics
             experiments to detect differentially expressed genes between two
             conditions},
  journal = {PLOS Computational Biology},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1371/journal.pcbi.1013293},
  note    = {Cited for the existence of spatial power analysis, its bootstrap
             rather than parametric framework, and its treatment of the number of
             tissue slices (not spots) as sample size. R package on CRAN; Shiny
             application available.}
}

@article{spatial_design_2024,
  title   = {Optimizing the design of spatial genomic studies},
  year    = {2024},
  note    = {PMC11166654. Cited for structured batch experimental design and the
             observation that parallel axis-aligned sections yield redundant or
             correlated information. Relevant because sectioning is destructive
             and the decision is not recoverable.}
}

@article{ffpe_ff_cardiac_2023,
  title   = {Comparison of whole transcriptome sequencing of fresh, frozen, and
             formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded cardiac tissue},
  journal = {PLOS ONE},
  year    = {2023},
  doi     = {10.1371/journal.pone.0283159},
  note    = {Cited for FFPE median RIN 2.5 and DV200 48 percent against RIN 8.1
             and DV200 96-97 percent for fresh and frozen; for falsely elevated
             mitochondrial transcripts in FFPE; and for the explicit
             recommendation not to compare expression across storage conditions.
             One tissue type: magnitudes are not universal.}
}

@misc{vai_spatial_specs,
  title        = {Spatial Collaborative Services: platform specifications},
  howpublished = {Van Andel Institute core facility documentation},
  note         = {Cited for platform sample requirements: Visium fresh frozen
                  RIN above 7.0 and FFPE DV200 above 50 percent; Xenium optimal
                  section thickness 5 um FFPE and 10 um fresh frozen. A core
                  facility's restatement of vendor specifications, not a primary
                  vendor document. Re-verify against current 10x documentation
                  before relying on any threshold.}
}

@article{ffpe_st_benchmark_2023,
  title   = {Benchmarking robust spatial transcriptomics approaches to capture
             the molecular landscape of archival FFPE tissue},
  year    = {2023},
  note    = {PREPRINT, bioRxiv, not confirmed peer reviewed at time of citation.
             Cited for FFPE block age driving RNA quality: blocks from 2018
             averaging DV200 around 70 percent against 31 percent for 2008
             blocks, and for lower initial RNA quality yielding decreased unique
             gene counts. Skin tissue.}
}

% ------------------------------------------------- batch 3: panel and codebook

@article{merfish_subcellular_2019,
  title   = {Spatial transcriptome profiling by MERFISH reveals subcellular RNA
             compartmentalization and cell cycle-dependent gene expression},
  journal = {Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences},
  year    = {2019},
  doi     = {10.1073/pnas.1912459116},
  note    = {Cited for the Hamming distance rule: HD >= 2 permits error detection,
             HD >= 3 additionally permits correction; and for a 69-bit HD4 HW4
             code imaging approximately 10,000 genes over 23 rounds of three-colour
             imaging.}
}

@article{merfish_plus_2025,
  title   = {MERFISH+, a large-scale multi-omics spatial technology},
  year    = {2025},
  note    = {PMC12637541. Cited for a 48-bit HW4 codebook carrying an 1,835-gene
             panel with roughly 75 adaptor probes pooled per round; for retaining
             clusters of at least three spots to allow single-bit error
             correction; and for the gene-to-blank ratio filter applied until a
             5 percent misidentification rate was reached.}
}

@article{merfish_codebook_2025,
  title   = {Boosting multiplexing capabilities for error-robust spatial
             transcriptomics},
  journal = {Science Advances},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1126/sciadv.adr4026},
  note    = {PMID 40315316. Cited for fixed Hamming weight as the mechanism that
             equalises detection sensitivity across genes; for weight four being
             chosen high enough to encode many genes without inflating molecular
             density; and for optimal codebook generation being an unsolved
             mathematical problem for most experimental parameters.}
}

@article{mouse_brain_atlas_2023,
  title   = {Molecularly defined and spatially resolved cell atlas of the whole
             mouse brain},
  journal = {Nature},
  year    = {2023},
  doi     = {10.1038/s41586-023-06808-9},
  note    = {Cited for a codebook including 116 blank barcodes assigned to no gene
             in order to measure the false-positive rate, and for a later
             1,147-gene panel assigning its 25 additional genes barcodes drawn
             from that blank set.}
}

@article{bioimaging_review_2023,
  title   = {Introduction to bioimaging-based spatial multi-omic novel methods},
  journal = {Quantitative Biology},
  year    = {2023},
  doi     = {10.15302/J-QB-023-0332},
  note    = {Cited for reported MERFISH error rates of approximately 10 percent
             false negative and 4 percent false positive, and for probe-design
             advice to avoid housekeeping genes, histones and very highly
             expressed genes. Review article: treat the error rates as indicative
             of a study, not as platform specifications.}
}

@misc{pachter_current_techs,
  title        = {Current era technologies},
  howpublished = {Museum of Spatial Transcriptomics, Pachter lab},
  note         = {Cited for optical crowding mitigations: imaging the most highly
                  expressed genes separately by single-molecule FISH rather than
                  including them in the combinatorial scheme, as in the first
                  MERFISH study, and the fact that this becomes laborious as their
                  number grows. Community resource, continuously updated: cite the
                  version consulted.}
}
